Your Own Preset and Plugin
**************************

.[perex]
Package the style you want on all your projects as a preset and your own rules as a plugin with a section of decisions, then share both through Composer. A project turns them on with a single line in the key `use`, for all of its code or just a part of it.


A Preset in NEON
================

A preset is a NEON file shaped like a configuration. It carries decisions, the `use` key listing what it builds on, and possibly `severity` and `suppressionComments`; nothing more. Here is the house style of a company called Acme:

```neon
# Acme house style
#
# PER Coding Style with tabs, lines of up to 100 characters, and the messages of exceptions as sentences.

use: perCs

file:
	lineLength: 100

indentation:
	unit: tab

expressions:
	comparison:
		# a comparison reads in the order it is spoken
		yoda: forbidden

acme:
	exceptionMessagePeriod: required
```

- A preset starts with a comment: the name on the first line, and below it what the preset is. A comment above a key says why the decision has its value; the [standards and sets |php-coding-standards] of DressCode are written this way too.
- `use` names the presets yours builds on, whether standards, sets or other presets. They lie below your preset in the order they are written, each one only once, so what your preset writes wins over what it builds on, and the project above it wins over the preset. Values are merged the same way as in the other [layers of the configuration |configuration#Layers].
- The decisions of a preset can belong to the engine as well as to a plugin, like `acme.exceptionMessagePeriod` in the example; the project must then have the plugin; otherwise the catalogue does not know the key.
- A preset cannot make the choices that belong to the project. The facts about the project (`targets`, `typeAnalysis`, `namespaces`, `nameResolution`), the scope of the run (`paths`, `excludePaths` and others), `fixRisky`, plugins and `rules` are left to the project. DressCode rejects a preset that sets them, with the error <code>''Preset `acme/house` sets `fixRisky`, which the project decides, not a preset.''</code> But a preset may use the `severity` key to make violations of a decision only warnings, like `file.longLines: warning` in the `psr12` standard.
- A preset meant to be added to any standard is a **set**: it does not decide the layout of the code, does not build on a standard, and does not touch spaces, indentation, line breaking or order. Say so in its opening comment.

A preset does not have to worry about the PHP version or about what the project has installed: a decision whose rule asks for a PHP version or a package in `requires` is silently skipped where the project does not have them. In the same way, a decision whose rule needs types is silently skipped in a project without `typeAnalysis: phpstan`.

A project uses a preset by putting the path to its file in `use`, for example `use: tools/house.neon`, or its name when a [plugin |#A Plugin with Rules and Presets] brings it. Because it is a layer like any other, you can also use it just for a part of the project, in an [override |configuration#Different Values for Part of the Project]:

```neon
use:
	- Acme\CodeStyle\Plugin    # knows the decisions acme.*, see below
	- perCs

overrides:
	-
		paths: [src]
		use: [tools/house.neon]
```


A Plugin with Rules and Presets
===============================

A plugin is what a package brings to a project: a class implementing `DressCode\Plugin`, whose `getManifest()` method returns a `PluginManifest`. The manifest lists the rules, the presets with their names and files, the **section** that holds the decisions of the plugin's rules, excluded paths and the address of the rule pages `ruleUrl`; the text below the example describes what else it can carry. Here is the plugin of the Acme package:

```php
namespace Acme\CodeStyle;

use DressCode\PluginManifest;


final class Plugin implements \DressCode\Plugin
{
	public function getManifest(): PluginManifest
	{
		return new PluginManifest(
			rules: [ExceptionMessagePeriodRule::class, NoDebugCallsRule::class],
			presets: ['acme/house' => __DIR__ . '/house.neon'],
			section: 'acme',
			excludePaths: ['generated'],
			ruleUrl: 'https://acme.dev/rules/{slug}',
		);
	}
}
```

The **section** is the name of the plugin in the configuration. All the decisions of its rules lie under it, `acme.exceptionMessagePeriod` and `acme.debugCalls`, so they do not clash with the engine, with another plugin or with the project's rules in the `project` section. A plugin that brings rules must have a section, and it must not be a reserved key or a section of the engine. Users then write the decisions of the plugin just like any others, see them next to findings, suppress them with `dresscode:ignore acme.debugCalls`, accept their risky fixes in `fixRisky` and have them explained by `dresscode explain acme`.

**Shared decision.** When two rules of a plugin with independent logic enforce one thing, for example debugging output in a call and in a comment, the manifest declares the decision in the key `decisions` and both rules name it in their attribute; the user then writes a single key that turns on both. [A Rule in Detail |rule-contract#A Shared Decision] describes how.

Presets from the manifest get their names in the form `vendor/name`, so the project uses them by name in `use` (`acme/house`) or for a single run with `--use`. `ruleUrl` holds the address of the rule pages with the placeholder `{slug}`, which stands for the class name of the rule without the `Rule` suffix and with a lowercase first letter; `dresscode explain` prints it for every decision of the rule. The excluded paths are added to those of the project. The manifest can also carry the file filter `skipWhen`, which skips a file even when the project has its own filter, analyses (when the project lists an analysis of the same class as the plugin, the project's analysis applies) and the plugins it builds on: such a plugin lies below it, and each class is used only once.

Notice what is **not** in the manifest: a plugin gives no decision a value and does not set a style, because `PluginManifest` has no such key at all; `decisions` only declares decisions. The rules of the plugin turn on only when the project, or a preset the project uses, writes their decisions. Which style applies is up to the project, so you can always read it in the project's own file:

```neon
use:
	- Acme\CodeStyle\Plugin
	- acme/house

acme:
	debugCalls: forbidden

mistakes:
	debugOutputFunctions: [dump]
```

The rules of a plugin may also read the keys of the engine: the rule behind `acme.debugCalls` takes the list of debugging functions from the engine parameter `mistakes.debugOutputFunctions`, so the project writes it once for all rules (see [A Rule in Detail |rule-contract#Values in the Rule]).

This is exactly how the `dresscode/upgrade-nette` package works: its plugin has the `nette` section with decisions such as `nette.linkArguments` or the map `nette.flagsAsNamedArguments`, and the package carries its upgrade data next to it, in the `upgrading/` directory.

A plugin goes into the `use` key of the project's configuration, next to the presets, by its class or as an [entity |configuration#Objects and Callbacks] with arguments; neither a preset nor an override can list it, because it makes rules and presets available to the whole project (see [Configuration |configuration#Plugins]). A plugin is the right fit where a package carries the settings and the project only turns them on. A project that only wants its own `skipWhen` filter, analysis or rule with dependencies needs no plugin: it writes them directly in `dresscode.neon` or `dresscode.php`.


A Composer Package
==================

A package with a preset or with rules is an ordinary Composer package that requires `dresscode/dresscode` and shares the autoloader with the project. It announces its plugin in `composer.json` under the key `extra.dresscode.plugin`, so after installation the project does not need to know its class and lets it in by the name of the package in `use`:

```json
{
	"name": "acme/code-style",
	"require": {
		"dresscode/dresscode": "^1.0"
	},
	"autoload": {
		"psr-4": {"Acme\\CodeStyle\\": "src/"}
	},
	"extra": {
		"dresscode": {
			"plugin": "Acme\\CodeStyle\\Plugin"
		}
	}
}
```

Besides `plugin`, DressCode recognizes two more keys under `extra.dresscode`, `upgrading-dir` and `packages`, with data for [upgrading libraries |replacement-maps#Data Files]; it reports any other key as a warning and skips it, because the package may be newer than the DressCode that reads it. Inside the package are the rule classes, the preset files, the plugin, and the fixtures with tests. The names of presets carry the vendor of the package (`acme/…`) and the decisions carry the section of the plugin, so that they do not clash with others. The user then installs the package, writes its name into `use` and chooses everything else in the configuration:

```neon
use:
	- perCs
	- acme/code-style
	- acme/house
```

The name of a package is not a preset: it lets in the package's plugin, whose code is loaded only when the project names it ([Configuration |configuration#Plugins] explains why). So tell your users in the instructions of the package to add its name to `use`; for an installed package that `use` does not name, the run reports the warning <code>''Package `acme/code-style` brings the plugin `Acme\CodeStyle\Plugin`, which loads only where `use` names the package or the plugin; skipped.''</code> Upgrade data under the key `upgrading` is loaded even when `use` does not name the package, because it carries no code.

